  "reason": "The records establish several important pieces separately: rapid cooling suppresses secondary/HCP phases and preserves single-phase BCC HfNbTaTiZr (R2); approximately 1 wt.% oxygen strongly hardens that exact alloy (R13, R19), while interstitial oxygen can strengthen BCC refractory alloys (R18); and silicide coatings can be formed on a closely related refractory HEA (R9). They also provide general support for selective oxygen affinity, oxide-film protection, and oxygen-derived strengthening. However, none points to extrusion printing of a hydride/metal ink, hydrogen de-binding, sequential hydride decomposition governed by formation enthalpies, or use of a titanium-sponge getter. Nor do they disclose the specific self-healing filling action of Zr/Hf oxides in a silicide coating. Thus the literature points toward the phase-control, oxygen-strengthening, and coating concepts, but reconstructing the integrated hydride-printing and in-situ-alloying process would require a substantial new idea."
